# Exact cached post-live --all findings; answers explain only the anchors of their class.

[step] This rail top at Z 6.44 is intentionally 0.24 mm above the basin's Z 6.20 top. The fixed rail is 3.60 mm wide and 2.40 mm thick, Z 4.04–6.44; its exact top is the common datum for the three solid-skin upper-release comparisons. The prepared common 0.24 mm layer grid deposits the last fixed rail at nozzle 6.44. This step is a contact datum, not an accidental smear of stock.
alternatives: Raising or thickening the rail changes the three roof schedules unless every eye is moved with it, requiring a new captive-gap/travel comparison. A flush basin-height rail changes the reference Z contact datum. A separately assembled larger rail removes the print-in-place bridge and changes the release question.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/print-assemblies/pip-base.step
click: x=-49.000 y=64.000 z=6.440

[step] These shallow 13.00 by 3.30 mm covered/revealed writing/release datum recesses have floors at Z 6.08, 0.12 mm below the 6.20 mm basin top. Their 0.12 mm side bands are faces cut into stock with 6.08 mm backing, not free-standing 0.12 mm webs. The moving handle can cover/reveal the datum; it is separate from the rail/eye release planes and carries no capture load.
alternatives: Leaving the full 6.20 mm surface provides larger continuous stock but removes the shallow revealed datum. A 0.24 mm deeper recess is more pronounced on the native layer grid but changes the datum surface/step. A separate writable card provides more room without this recess, but is detached from the moving shutter.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/print-assemblies/pip-base.step
click: x=-49.000 y=56.650 z=6.080

[step] These exact picks are the mirrored underside POUR V2 / MASTER 2026-10-11 identities recessed 0.48 mm into the 6.20 mm basin. The smallest readings are remaining letter terminals and counters at the recess boundary, not the basin's nominal section. Both identities are recognizable in the current native emitted-width overlays recorded in lettering-review.json; physical legibility is unrecorded.
alternatives: Larger lettering or wider counters can make the residual islands larger, but require a wider underside identity field. Filling the counters or using a stencil font removes these small enclosed details while changing the reference lettering. A raised underside label needs another bed pose or a stand-off to keep its first surface printable.
